node_modules/
dist/
.turbo/
*.tsbuildinfo
.compile-state.json
.DS_Store
*.log
.env
.env.local
# direnv's shell file. It carries live credentials BY DESIGN — that is what direnv is for — so it
# is per-machine operator state, never repo state. Without this rule a `git add -A` sweeps it into
# a commit, which is exactly how it once reached history and had to be scrubbed back out.
.envrc
.forge/local/
.claude/worktrees/
# The projected corpus, one subdirectory per harness (`cratylus project --harness <h>`).
#
# A PROJECTION IS NOT A DEPLOYMENT. The render goes to a NEUTRAL staging dir that Claude
# Code does NOT auto-load — deliberately NOT a `<repo>/.claude/{agents,skills}` tree, since
# a project-scoped session would load that AS a de-facto deployment merely because it was
# rendered. Scope is an accident applied at DEPLOY time, never a render side effect. The
# real target is user scope, `~/.claude` per host; a `<repo>/.claude/{agents,skills}` tree
# may exist only as an explicit project-scope opt-in by a downstream adopter.
#
# NAMED FOR ITS PRODUCER, which is the convention every tool in this class already follows
# — `.next/`, `.nuxt/`, `.svelte-kit/`, `.astro/`, `.turbo/`. It is NOT `dist/`: `dist` is
# the publishable library (`files: ["dist"]`, `clean: rm -rf dist`, turbo
# `build.outputs`), singular per package and shipped to npm. This is a generated corpus for
# deployment, plural per harness, and shipped nowhere.
#
# The harnesses are SUBDIRECTORIES rather than sibling dirs distinguished by suffix. The
# previous names were `.render-ts` and `.render-ts-codex`, where `-ts` meant "the
# TypeScript render, as opposed to the Python one" — and the Python one (`resolve.py`,
# `deploy.py`, `packages/canon/ideas`) has not existed for some time. The discriminator
# named a completed migration, and the concatenated-suffix shape is what let the codex tree
# drift undetected. `harness-parity.test.ts` compares the two; siblings under one parent is
# the shape that comparison actually has.
#
# The oracle is deliberately NOT in here. `.cratylus/` is wholly derived and safe to
# `rm -rf`; `packages/canon/.render-oracle` is a TRACKED attestation over these bytes, and
# a tracked file inside a disposable directory is a file that gets destroyed by a clean.
.cratylus/
# Memory's per-repo config: scope markers + per-host $HOME. OPERATOR-AUTHORED and
# host-specific, so local-only. Committed template: .cratylus.memory.json.example.
# Why this name (and not the runtime dotfile's): packages/memory/src/node.ts.
.cratylus.memory.json
!.cratylus.memory.json.example
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.scratchpad/
# Agent memory stores are per-agent home state — never repo-tracked (raw EPISODIC capture is agent-home only).
EPISODIC.jsonl
MEMORY.md
SELF.md
# graphify knowledge-graph. Track ONLY graph.json — the single file with explicit track-intent
# (.gitattributes merge=graphify; a merge driver is meaningless for an ignored file). Everything else
# under graphify-out/ is regenerable from it or ephemeral (GRAPH_REPORT.md, graph.html, cost.json,
# manifest.json, the .graphify_* working index, cache/) — rebuilt by `graphify update`, kept out of git
# to avoid churn/bloat. packages/graphify-out/ is a stray second graph (root resolved to packages/).
graphify-out/*
!graphify-out/graph.json
packages/graphify-out/

# Release staging: tarballs packed for audit-then-upload. Regenerated every run.
.pack/
